WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.658
Length validation is central in C because
input length often affects copying,

2
00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:07.635
indexing, allocation, parsing, and string
handling.

3
00:00:07.715 --> 00:00:11.369
A length value should be checked before it
drives a memory operation.

4
00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:15.229
It should also be checked against the
exact operation being performed,

5
00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:18.081
not a convenient limit from another part
of the code.

6
00:00:18.161 --> 00:00:21.121
String length and buffer capacity are
different facts.

7
00:00:21.201 --> 00:00:23.469
String length describes current content.

8
00:00:23.549 --> 00:00:26.888
Capacity describes the available storage
in the destination.

9
00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:28.165
A copy, append, parse,

10
00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:31.773
or formatted output operation may need
room for data, separators,

11
00:00:31.853 --> 00:00:33.679
and a terminating null character.

12
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:36.342
The validation rule must account for all
of that.

13
00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:39.005
Maximum accepted input size should be
explicit.

14
00:00:39.085 --> 00:00:43.180
A program should know how much input it is
willing to accept for a field and why.

15
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.165
Limits protect memory use, processing
time, log size, storage behavior,

16
00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:48.820
and downstream assumptions.

17
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.995
Limits should be enforced before
allocation or before a long parse step where

18
00:00:53.075 --> 00:00:53.705
practical.

19
00:00:53.785 --> 00:00:57.187
Array bounds and index validation should
happen before access.

20
00:00:57.267 --> 00:01:01.172
A value read from input may be a count,
offset, index, length, or capacity.

21
00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:02.953
Those are not interchangeable.

22
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:06.183
Code should check that a value is
nonnegative where needed,

23
00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:10.295
within the correct maximum, and consistent
with the object it will address.

24
00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:13.461
Size values should remain trustworthy
after validation.

25
00:01:13.541 --> 00:01:16.943
If the program validates a length and then
transforms the input,

26
00:01:17.023 --> 00:01:20.362
appends to a buffer, decodes text, or
changes representation,

27
00:01:20.442 --> 00:01:23.088
the old length may no longer describe the
new data.

28
00:01:23.168 --> 00:01:27.640
Reviewers should track when size metadata
must be recalculated or carried forward.

29
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.941
The practical review question is: what
exact operation will this size control?

30
00:01:32.021 --> 00:01:34.919
A check for display length may not protect
allocation.

31
00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:38.400
A check for source length may not protect
destination capacity.

32
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.260
Safe validation ties bounds to the real
memory operation that follows.
